\begin{abstract}
\noindent\textbf{Background and Objective:}
Fully coupled three-dimensional (3D) fluid--structure interaction (FSI) simulations can quantify vessel-wall stress under pulsatile hemodynamic loading, but their high computational cost limits repeated patient-specific evaluations and sensitivity analyses. This study aimed to develop a snapshot-based framework for efficient estimation of localized vessel-wall stress at selected cardiac phases, particularly in vascular models with limited cyclic wall deformation.

\noindent\textbf{Methods:}
The proposed framework integrates a one-dimensional reduced-order hemodynamic model with steady 3D fluid and structural analyses. Pulsatile pressure and flow-rate waveforms were first predicted using the reduced-order model informed by limited 3D solution data. Boundary conditions were then extracted at representative cardiac phases and applied to steady 3D fluid simulations to reconstruct wall traction. The computed wall traction was transferred to steady structural analyses to estimate vessel-wall stress. The framework was evaluated against fully coupled pulsatile three-dimensional FSI simulations in an idealized stenosed coronary model, a subject-specific coronary artery model, and a subject-specific abdominal aortic aneurysm model.

\noindent\textbf{Results:}
Across all cases, the proposed method reproduced von Mises stress fields with low spatial relative $L_2$-norm error at the selected cardiac phases. The maximum relative $L_2$-norm stress error was below 1\% in the idealized coronary model, below 5\% in the subject-specific coronary model, and below 2\% in the aneurysm model. Equivalent strain showed larger, localized discrepancies, particularly near complex geometric regions and phases associated with rapid wall motion. The proposed framework reduced computational cost by more than 30-fold compared with fully coupled three-dimensional FSI simulations.

\noindent\textbf{Conclusions:}
The snapshot-based framework provides a computationally efficient, stress-oriented strategy for selected-phase vessel-wall assessment under pulsatile hemodynamic loading. The method may support repeated patient-specific biomechanical analyses when fully coupled transient FSI simulations are computationally prohibitive.
\end{abstract}

