\section{Introduction}
\label{Introduction}

Cardiovascular computational modeling has become an essential tool for the quantitative analysis and prediction of complex physiological phenomena~\cite{colebank2024guidelines, macraild2024accelerated}. The cardiovascular system is inherently nonlinear and dynamically coupled, with interactions among flow, pressure, vessel-wall deformation, and upstream and downstream vascular responses governing overall hemodynamics. In particular, vascular tissues are subjected to pulsatile loading generated by the cardiac cycle, leading to continuous adaptation in response to physiological and metabolic demands. As a result, conventional image-based simulations assuming rigid vessel walls are often insufficient to characterize the underlying biomechanical environment~\cite{gu2024comprehensive, schahab2025abdominal}. Accordingly, integrated hemodynamic and biomechanical quantities should be evaluated under dynamic pulsatile conditions, particularly when wall deformation and structural stress are of interest.

Fluid–structure interaction (FSI) analyses are therefore widely employed in cardiovascular biomechanics to evaluate hemodynamic and biomechanical quantities in an integrated manner~\cite{schahab2025abdominal, lopes2019influence, athani2024fsi_review, morab2024computational, saib2025fsi_review, debruyne1994cfr, pijls1995ffr, mcgarvey2026lesion, pedrigi2014stress, Cheng1993, li2007structural, wang2025role, marcinno2024computational}. Despite these advantages, high-resolution three-dimensional (3D) pulsatile simulations, particularly fully coupled FSI analyses, remain computationally demanding. This computational burden limits their practical use in large-scale patient cohort studies and repeated simulations for uncertainty quantification, sensitivity analysis, and patient-specific computational assessment~\cite{athani2024fsi_review, lissoni2025computational}.

To address these challenges, several strategies have been proposed to reduce the computational cost of pulsatile three-dimensional (3D) simulations. Rigid-wall computational fluid dynamics (CFD) models are widely used due to their simplicity and efficiency; however, they neglect vessel-wall deformation and therefore cannot directly provide structural stress fields~\cite{lopes2019influence, athani2024fsi_review}. One-way or weakly coupled approaches improve efficiency by decoupling fluid and structural analyses, but still require transient 3D simulations to obtain time-resolved loading conditions~\cite{syed2023fsi_review}. Reduced-order hemodynamic models, including one-dimensional (1D) and lumped parameter representations, offer substantial computational savings and can capture global pulsatile behavior~\cite{shi2011review, macraild2024accelerated}. However, these models do not directly provide localized 3D vessel-wall stress distributions required for structural assessment. More recently, surrogate and machine-learning-based approaches have been introduced to accelerate high-fidelity simulations~\cite{pegolotti2024learning, barzegar2025surrogate, grieco2026wss}. While effective, these methods typically require extensive high-fidelity training data and additional validation efforts.

Despite these developments, computational approaches that efficiently estimate localized 3D vessel-wall stress at selected cardiac phases, without repeated fully coupled FSI simulations or pre-trained surrogate models, remains limited.

Motivated by this need, the present study develops a snapshot-based coupling framework for efficient estimation of vessel-wall stress under pulsatile hemodynamic loading. The proposed approach integrates a 1D reduced-order hemodynamic model with steady 3D fluid and structural analyses. The 1D model is first calibrated using limited 3D solution data and then used to predict pulsatile pressure and flow-rate waveforms. Representative time points are selected from these waveforms, and the corresponding boundary pressure and flow conditions are extracted and applied to steady 3D fluid simulations to reconstruct localized wall traction. The resulting traction is then transferred to steady structural analyses to estimate vessel-wall stress at the selected cardiac phases.

The proposed methodology is evaluated against fully coupled 3D FSI simulations in an idealized coronary artery model, a subject-specific coronary artery model, and a subject-specific abdominal aortic aneurysm model. The results demonstrate that the framework achieves more than a 30-fold reduction in computational cost while reproducing localized von Mises stress fields with low global error compared with fully coupled 3D FSI simulations.

\section{Methods}
\label{Methods}
\subsection{Proposed snapshot-based coupling framework}

The proposed framework provides a computationally efficient approximation of localized vessel-wall stress at selected cardiac phases while avoiding repeated fully coupled 3D pulsatile FSI simulations. The method consists of three sequential steps.

First, a 1D pulsatile flow simulation based on a ROM is performed to compute the temporal waveforms of pressure and flow rate, denoted by $P(t)$ and $Q(t)$, throughout the vascular tree, including the inlet and outlet boundaries. From these waveforms, boundary condition values at a specific time point $t_a$, namely $P_{\mathrm{BC}}(t_a)$ and $Q_{\mathrm{BC}}(t_a)$, are extracted.

Second, the extracted boundary conditions are applied to a 3D steady-state fluid simulation. Although temporally steady, this simulation preserves the geometric complexity of the vascular domain and enables computation of the total wall traction, defined as the sum of pressure and viscous shear stress acting on the luminal surface.

Finally, a 3D steady structural analysis of the vessel wall is performed by prescribing the computed wall traction as a boundary condition. The resulting stress field is interpreted as an approximation of the structural response corresponding to the selected time point $t_a$ under pulsatile physiological loading. An overview of the proposed snapshot-based coupling strategy is provided in Fig.~\ref{fig:fig1}.

\subsubsection{One-dimensional reduced-order hemodynamics}

Pulsatile blood flow is computed by solving the following 1D governing equations under the assumption of a rigid vessel wall:
\begin{gather}
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} = 0, \\
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial t}
+ \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \left( \alpha \frac{Q^{2}}{S} \right)
+ \frac{S}{\rho}\frac{\partial P}{\partial z}
= N \frac{Q}{S},
\end{gather}
where $S$ denotes the cross-sectional area, $z$ is the axial coordinate, $\alpha$ is the momentum correction coefficient, and $N$ represents the viscous resistance term. A parabolic velocity profile is assumed, with $\alpha = 4/3$ and $N = 8\pi\nu$.

While these equations efficiently capture global pulsatile hemodynamics, they are limited in representing pressure losses in diseased or geometrically complex regions, such as stenoses and bifurcations. To address this limitation, the ROM coefficients are adjusted using limited 3D solution data by modeling pressure drop as a nonlinear function of the volumetric flow rate~\cite{choi2025developing}:
\begin{align}
\Delta P = a Q + b Q^2 + c \frac{dQ}{dt},\label{ROM_equation}
\end{align}
where $Q$ denotes the volumetric flow rate. The coefficients $a$, $b$, and $c$ are identified based on minimal 3D solution data~\cite{choi2025developing}. The resulting ROM provides rapid, accurate computation of pressure and flow waveforms for various pulsatile conditions.

\subsubsection{Snapshot selection and steady fluid--structure coupling}

Using the ROM, pressure and flow-rate waveforms can be efficiently obtained at arbitrary spatial locations under prescribed pulsatile conditions. Based on these waveforms, representative time instants, denoted by $t_a$, are selected to capture key phases associated with significant structural response.

For each selected time point, a steady three-dimensional fluid simulation is performed by prescribing the corresponding boundary conditions extracted from the ROM. Although temporally steady, this formulation preserves the geometric fidelity of the domain and enables efficient evaluation of wall loading. The resulting wall traction is defined as:


\begin{align}
\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{wall}}(t_a) = \boldsymbol{\sigma}_f(t_a)\mathbf{n}
\quad \text{on } \Gamma_{\mathrm{wall}}.
\end{align}

The computed wall traction is then transferred to the corresponding surface of the solid domain and applied as a boundary condition for structural analysis. This transfer is performed using a surface mapping procedure to ensure consistency between the fluid and solid discretizations. The structural problem is solved under a quasi-static assumption, with fixed inlet and outlet boundaries, leading to:
\begin{align}
\nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}_s + \mathbf{f}_s = 0
\quad \text{in } \Omega_s.
\end{align}

This framework assumes that the instantaneous fluid-induced traction provides the dominant contribution to the stress field at each selected time point. The influence of the neglected transient coupling effects was assessed by comparison with fully coupled FSI simulations. Accordingly, the method is designed to approximate instantaneous stress states rather than the full transient dynamics of the coupled system.

\subsubsection{Three-dimensional governing equations at selected time instants}

Three-dimensional fluid and structural analyses were performed by solving the incompressible Navier--Stokes equations and continuity equation in the fluid domain, and Cauchy’s equation of motion in the solid domain~\cite{zhu2022svfsi}.

For the reference fully coupled FSI simulations, the fluid and solid domains are coupled using an arbitrary Lagrangian--Eulerian (ALE) formulation, which enables consistent treatment of mesh deformation and fluid--structure interaction at the interface~\cite{zhu2022svfsi}.

The vessel wall is modeled as an isotropic linear elastic material. Although vascular tissues exhibit nonlinear, anisotropic, and hyperelastic behavior~\cite{masson2011carotid}, linear elasticity has been widely adopted in computational biomechanics due to its numerical robustness and reduced parametric uncertainty~\cite{lopes2019influence, li2022finite, shakya2024fluid, benitez2021evaluating}. In the present study, this simplified constitutive model is employed to isolate and evaluate the performance of the proposed computational framework, rather than to capture detailed material behavior.

